      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the probability of drawing a 6 first and then a Queen from a standard deck of 52 cards. We will use the concept of conditional probability and the independence of drawing cards without replacement.\n\n1. **Calculate the probability of drawing a 6 first:**\n   There are 4 sixes in a standard deck of 52 cards. Therefore, the probability of drawing a 6 first is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{52} = \\frac{1}{13}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the probability of drawing a Queen second given that a 6 was drawn first:**\n   After drawing a 6, there are now 51 cards left in the deck. There are 4 hearts, 4 clubs, 4 spades, and 4 diamonds, making a total of 13 Ques. Therefore, the probability of drawing a Queen second is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Second card is a Queen} \\mid \\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{4}{51}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the combined probability:**\n   Since the events are dependent (the loss of a card affects the probability of the second draw), we multiply the probabilities of the two events:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{First card is a 6 and second card is a Queen}) = P(\\text{First card is a 6}) \\times P(\\text{Second card is a Queen} \\mid \\text{First card is a 6}) = \\frac{1}{13} \\times \\frac{4}{51} = \\frac{4}{663}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the probability that the first card is a 6 and the second card is a Queen is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{4}{663}}\\).",
